  "id": "T1556_003_txt_4",
  "content": "Adversaries may modify pluggable authentication modules (PAM) to access user credentials or enable otherwise unwarranted access to accounts. PAM is a modular system of configuration files, libraries, and executable files which guide authentication for many services. The most common authentication module is pam_unix.so , which retrieves, sets, and verifies account authentication information in /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow . [1] [2] [3] Adversaries may modify components of the PAM system to create backdoors. PAM components, such as pam_unix.so , can be patched to accept arbitrary adversary supplied values as legitimate credentials. [4] Malicious modifications to the PAM system may also be abused to steal credentials. Adversaries may infect PAM resources with code to harvest user credentials, since the values exchanged with PAM components may be plain-text since PAM does not store passwords. [5] [1]",
  "category": "Article",
